A||N||4||DaveL||Performance coils||||1||
Z||000000||DaveL||02-17-2000||04:05 PM||||Does anyone make higher performance ignition coils for 2.5?||||reg||1
Z||000001||mwbackus||02-17-2000||04:21 PM||||<BR>MSD of course.  The MSD DIS-2 system is specifcally designed for distributorless ignitions such as those used in the newer L4's.  <P> <A HREF="http://www.msdignition.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.msdignition.com/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||02-17-2000||06:37 PM||||which motor....<P>distributor motor parts are available from several people.<P>For DIS motors Coils are only available thru Accell. DIS-2 from MSD is not a coil but a capacitive discharge device that connects between the coil controller and the coil packs. As of last data available DIS-2 installation requires cutting the stock harness.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mwbackus||02-17-2000||06:59 PM||||The MSD DIS-2 "System" I was referring to consists of the following components:<P>MSD Digital DIS-2, PN 6211<P><BR>DIS-2 HO, 2-Coil Packs, PN 62111<P><BR>Coil Interface Module, PN 8870<P>Part number 62111 is for the "coil packs" for the DIS ignition.  More information on the Coil Interface Module can be found here:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.msdignition.com/2000_10.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.msdignition.com/2000_10.htm</A> <P>I believe this enables application of this system without cutting, since they state that it "connects directly to the factory terminals".<P><BR>Mike<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||02-17-2000||07:45 PM||||Well then they updated it since I talked to them last. They said they were working on it, but it sounded like a low priority item.<P>Before thinking about fooling with the coils try putting good plugs and wires on. Don't use Bosch Platinum. The Borg-Warner helical core wires that Pep Boys carries work well, and aren't very expencive.<P>DIS motors are really picky about the condition of plugs and wires. With the fairly rare exception of a failed coil nearly everything that goes wrong in DIS is plug or wire related. ((Bad plugs or wires can ruin a DIS coil!!!)) Sometimes the crank sensor goes, but that's not real common either.<P>considering that DIS already generates more energy than most distributor systems. I have to wonder what if any real benifit there is in altering it for a street car. The only time I can imagine the system needing any more power is under high compression or boost. If I was going to do anything to my own, I'd just replace the coils. Accell's coils aren't that much more than the OEM ones.||||reg||1
